var additionalData = [
["Pages/Page_162944432.html","1 Introduction",
"\t\t\n1 Introduction\nObjectives and Content\nYou must provide the following two types of application icons for Nintendo Switch™ (hereafter Switch).\n \tFor Switch\nMainly used on places like the splash screen, HOME Menu, and user page.\n \tFor smart device applications\nThis document provides an overview of Switch application icon images, including information about file formats and considerations needed when designing icons.\nIntended Audience\n \tSwitch application developers\n \tGraphic data designers\nApplication icons, title logos, main visuals, etc.\nWorking Environment\n \tAny environment where software like Adobe Illustrator CC and Adobe Photoshop CC can run.\n \tDevelopment environments where you can display application icons on Switch.\n \tEnvironment for Using AuthoringTool.exe or Authoring Editor\nNote: AuthoringTool.exe and Authoring Editor are automatically installed when you install the NintendoSDK using the Nintendo Dev Interface.\nWhen to Read This Document\nRead this document when you are developing Switch applications and creating NX application icons.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"]
,["Pages/Page_170066866.html","2 Application Icons",
"\t\t\n2 Application Icons\n Switch Application Icon Policy\nNintendo is using application icon as an easily understandable term, but we recommend a higher-density image for Switch that combines the application's main visual with logo, as you would see for a game software package.\nWe do not recommend the simpler images that were commonly used for the Nintendo 3DS and elsewhere.\n \t\n \tSample Application Icon Images \tDescription\n \t Recommended\n \t\n \tAn image that densely combines a main visual and a logo.\n \t Not Recommended\n \t\n \tA simple, plainer image.\nAs shown in the following image, you run the risk of an icon's excessive simplicity standing out if you use a simple, plainer icon.\n Note: The design of the Switch HOME Menu is not yet finalized.\nAvoid enlarging a low-resolution image and using it as an icon.\n \t\n \tSample Application Icon Images \tDescription\n \t Recommended\n \t\n \tAn image with an appropriate resolution.\n \t Not Recommended\n \t\n \tIcon images created from low-resolution images.\nFor more information about designing Switch application icons, see 3 Designing Application Icons.\n Note\n The sample application icon images used on this page are either from existing Wii U or Nintendo 3DS applications, or are artistic conceptions. They are not for applications that will be available for Switch.\nApplication Icons and Language Settings\nAn application icon can be specified for each language, and the icon that corresponds to the language and region configured in the system settings is displayed.\nFor more information, see How Display Languages Work in Nintendo Switch System Feature Guide Attachments.\nWhere to Use Application Icons and Their Sizes\nThe application icons are displayed in the HOME Menu, Entrance, and other areas. The size varies depending on where the icon is displayed.\nSupplemental Information About Application Icons for Demo Software\nWe recommend using a different application icon to make it easier to differentiate demo software when starting applications or when managing data.\nFor the various rules that applications must follow, see the Nintendo Switch Guidelines.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"]
,["Pages/Page_199794907.html","3 Designing Application Icons",
"\t\t\n3 Designing Application Icons\nThere are two types of application icons: those for use on Switch and those for use on smart devices.\nDesign Workflow\n \tProcedure \tDescription \tNotes\n \t1 \t Create an image in BMP format. (This image will be referred to as the original image.)\n \t―\n \t2 \tLoad the original image created in step 1 into Authoring Tool. \tImages for Switch and for smart devices are automatically created from the original image. You can also use the following format to design and set your own image for Switch.\n \t3 \tTest the appearance of the Switch icon on the NX development hardware. \t If you install the ROM created using Authoring Tool onto the NX development hardware, you can test the icon in the HOME Menu and others.\n Format Details\nOriginal Image\n \tSize \t 1024 pixels wide by 1024 pixels high\n \tResolution (DPI) \tNo restrictions\n \tSave Format \tBMP\n Exported Images From Authoring Tool\nAuthoring Tool is a command-line tool for creating ROMs from a program, application icon, or software legal information.\n \t \t For Switch \t For Smart Device Applications\n \tSize \t 256 pixels wide by 256 pixels high\n \t1024 pixels wide by 1024 pixels high\n \tResolution (DPI) \tNo restrictions \t72\n \tCapacity \tMax 100 KB \tNo restrictions\n \tSave Format \t JPEG\nNote: You cannot use the progressive JPEG format.\n \tJPEG\n \tNotes \t If the image that was automatically created by Authoring Tool does not meet the expected level of quality, you can directly specify an image for Switch that you designed in Authoring Tool.\n That image will be used as is for Switch in such cases. The automatically created image will only be used for smart device applications.\n Use the same image as that from the original when creating your own Switch image. Ensure that it meets these format requirements.\n Do not change things like the logo placement from the original image. Note that you cannot submit a master when different from the original image.\n \tYou cannot create and set this on your own.\n Appearance in the HOME Menu\nApplication Icon Layout Elements\n \tNumber \tElement \tDescription \tNotes\n \t a\n \tTitle Logo \tWe recommend the placement of a title logo. \t―\n \tMain Visual \t We recommend using main visuals that provide an impression of the application's content.\n \t Take care not to obscure any important information, such as logos or the faces of characters.\n Elements Overlaid on Application Icons\n \tNumber \tElement \tDescription \tNotes\n \t b \tPlaying display \t Automatically displays beneath the application icon when application is paused. \t \tMake sure that the user can identify the application icon even when the application is paused.\n \t The user icon is displayed when a user account is selected in the application.\nNote: When multiple users are selected at the same time, up to four user icons can be displayed.\nYou can check how the icons are displayed when no user account is selected or when multiple user accounts are selected at the same time using either the application_icon_format_XXXXXXXX.psd or application_icon_format_XXXXXXXX.ai file included with this documentation.\n Info\n When you create the original image for an application icon, you can use the following files, which include layouts for information such as display during pause: application_icon_format_XXXXXXXX.psd or application_icon_format_XXXXXXXX.ai.\nMasking\nApplication icons are automatically edited in certain cases. For example, the corners can be rounded depending on where the icons are used. Avoid placing any critical information at the corners or designing the icon based on specific outer shapes.\n Note\n Creators do not need to round the corners.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",0,"en-US"]
];
